## P2 — Which tool to use

| Action | Tool |
|--------|------|
| Find pages | `notion-search` (`query_type: internal`, `filters: {}`) |
| Read page | `notion-fetch` (pass page id from search) |
| New page | `notion-create-pages` (parent `page_id` + `properties.title` + Notion markdown `content`) |
| Append section | `notion-update-page` → `insert_content` at `end` |
| Edit section | `notion-fetch` first → `update_content` with exact `old_str` |
| Replace whole page | `notion-update-page` → `replace_content` (careful — confirm with user if child pages exist) |

**Markdown:** Fetch `notion://docs/enhanced-markdown-spec` resource before first write in a session if syntax is non-obvious. Use Notion `<table header-row="true">` for tables — **never** bare markdown pipe tables on Notion pages.

**Titles:** Set only in `properties.title` — do not repeat as `#` heading in content.

### Table layout (mandatory — user preference 2026-05-21)

Every Notion table must **span the full page content width** with columns sized to content length.

1. Use Notion-flavored HTML tables (not `| col | col |` markdown).
2. **Always** include `<colgroup>` with an explicit `width` on **every** `<col>`.
3. Set widths **proportional to the longest line** in each column (header + all body cells).
4. Target **760–820 px total** across columns (full content width). Minimum ~70 px per column.
5. Wider columns for long prose / code identifiers; narrower for Count, Dev %, flags.

**Template (2 columns — label + long description):**

```html
<table header-row="true">
<colgroup>
<col width="280">
<col width="520">
</colgroup>
<tr>
<td>Column A</td>
<td>Column B</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Short label</td>
<td>Longer explanatory text that should get the wider column</td>
</tr>
</table>
```

**Template (3+ columns):** same pattern — compute each `width` as `(max_chars_in_col / sum_max_chars) * 800`, round to integers, adjust so sum ≈ 800.

When editing an existing page, **`notion-fetch` first** — if tables lack `<colgroup>`, fix via `replace_content` or targeted `update_content`.

